%[text] %[text:anchor:T_4bf2] # Matrix Methods for Linear Systems
%[text] 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Linear regression](text:image:5779) ![Linear circuit](text:image:9a5f)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Left panel: linear regression - what straight line best captures the data?*
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Right panel: a linear circuit composed of resistors and DC power sources. How much current flows through each branch?*
%[text] Linear regression is one of the most broadly used mathematical techniques. It is ubiquitous in traditional fields, like physics and biology, and modern disciplines, like machine learning. You've probably used linear regression yourself. But do you know how it is computed? 
%[text] It's easy to determine the current flowing through a branch of a small linear circuit with few calculations. However, in complex linear circuits, the current flows through many connected paths, complicating the problem. So how can you compute the current flowing through each branch without pages of tedious algebra?
%[text] The answers to both questions lie in the matrix methods of linear algebra. In this live script, you will learn how to represent, analyze, and solve linear systems in matrix form – both by hand and using MATLAB functions.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] **Before you get started:**
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_H_BF646A47] This live script is intended to be used with the code hidden. On the **View** tab of the MATLAB toolstrip, in the **View** section, select **Hide Code**.  Alternately, select **Hide Code** using the icon ![live script code hidden icon](text:image:795b) at the top right of the Live Editor pane.
