%[text] %[text:anchor:H_289e] ## Systems of linear equations
%[text] **Definition**. A *linear equation* only contains terms that are either multiples of variables (e.g. $1.2x${"altText":"1.2x"}) or constants. That is, it can be expressed in the form:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $a\_1x\_1 + a\_2x\_2 + a\_3x\_3 +  \\ldots = b${"altText":"a\_1x\_1 + a\_2x\_2 + a\_3x\_3 + ... = b"}
%[text] where $a\_i${"altText":"a\_i"} and $b${"altText":"b"} are constants and each $x\_i${"altText":"x\_i"} is a variable.
%[text] **Definition**. A *system of linear equations* (also called a *linear system*) is a set of linear equations considered together.
%[text] - Linear systems can have a unique solution, an infinite set of solutions, or no solution.
%[text] - In this live script, only systems with equal numbers of variables and equations are examined. *Overdetermined* (more equations than unknowns) and *underdetermined* (fewer equations than unknowns) systems are not considered.
%[text] - A 3-dimensional linear system can be visualized as a set of three planes. The intersection of all three planes represents the solution set. \
%[text] ![](text:image:1754) **Example**. Consider the linear system
syms x y z a b c d
A = sym([0 1 1; 0 1 -2; a b c]);
bvec = sym([-1; 2; d]);
xvec = [x; y; z];
LinearSystem = A*xvec == bvec
  %[control:button:33d7]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] Depending on the values of the constants $a${"altText":"a"}, $b${"altText":"b"}, $c${"altText":"c"}, and $d${"altText":"d"}, this system may have a unique solution, no solutions, or infinite solutions.
a =1; % Default: 1 %[control:slider:9fd9]{"position":[4,5]}
b =-1; % Default: -1 %[control:slider:43d6]{"position":[4,6]}
c =-1; % Default: -1 %[control:slider:41c7]{"position":[4,6]}
d =-1; % Default: -1 %[control:slider:4896]{"position":[4,6]}
  %[control:button:1345]{"position":[1,2]}
A(3,:) = [a b c];
bvec(3) = d;
lims = [-6 6];
Plot3DSystem(A,bvec,lims); % This function plots the planes
%[text] ![](text:image:1a95) **Try**
%[text] - Set `a = -1`, `b = 0`, `c = 3`, and `d = 1` and plot the system.
%[text] - Hover over the plot to show the toolstrip and use the rotation tool ![](text:image:6993) to find a good angle to view the solution.
%[text] - Use the data tip tool ![](text:image:7fc1) to estimate the numeric value of the solution. Record your estimated solution in the space provided below. You will validate it against the analytic solution in the next exercise. \
MySltn = [""] %[control:editfield:0b85]{"position":[11,13]}
%[text] - Set `a = 0`, `b = -1`, `c = -3`, and `d = -2` and plot the system. What is the solution?
%[text] - Set `a = 0`, `b = -1`, `c = -3`, and `d = 3`. What is the solution? \
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:65e2) **Exercise**. Use elimination or substitution to solve the linear system
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\begin{array}{rl}\ny+z&=-1\\\\\ny-2\\,z&=2\\\\\n-x+3z&=1\n\\end{array}\\\n${"altText":"system of equations: y+z =-1; y-2 z =2; -x+3z =1"}
ShowSolution = false; %[control:checkbox:5536]{"position":[16,21]}
if(ShowSolution)
    A = [0 1 1; 0 1 -2; -1 0 3];
    b = [-1; 2; 1];
    xvec = A\b;
    x = xvec(1)
    y = xvec(2)
    z = xvec(3)
end
%[text] ![](text:image:6997) **Reflect.** Above, you graphically estimated the solution to be
if(exist("MySltn","var"))
    MySltn
else
    MySltn = ""
end
%[text] Were you close?
