%[text] %[text:anchor:H_2ea2] ### **Computing the matrix inverse by row reduction**
%[text] You can compute the inverse of a matrix $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} using row reduction operations. To do so:
%[text] 1\. Write the matrix together with an identity matrix as an augmented matrix: $(\\mathbf{A} \\ \\mathbf{I})${"altText":"AI"}. For example, for a 3x3 matrix this would be:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\left(\\begin{array}{ccc}\na\_{11} & a\_{12} & a\_{13}\\\\\na\_{21} & a\_{22} & a\_{23}\\\\\na\_{31} & a\_{32} & a\_{33}\\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\  \\ \\begin{array}{ccc}\n1 & 0 & 0 \\\\\n0 & 1 & 0 \\\\\n0 & 0 & 1 \n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"(matrix \[ a\_(11) , a\_(12) , a\_(13); a\_(21) , a\_(22) , a\_(23); a\_(31) , a\_(32) , a\_(33); \] matrix \[ 1 , 0 , 0 ; 0 , 1 , 0 ; 0 , 0 , 1 \])"}
%[text] 2\. Use row operations to convert $\\mathbf A${"altText":"A"} into the identity matrix. The operations are encoded in the right-side matrix, producing $\\mathbf A^{-1}${"altText":"A^(-1)"}.
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\left(\\begin{array}{ccc}\n1 & 0 & 0 \\\\\n0 & 1 & 0 \\\\\n0 & 0 & 1 \n\\end{array}\n\\  \\ \\begin{array}{ccc}\n &  &  \\\\\n & \\mathbf{A}^{-1} &  \\\\\n &  & \n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"(matrix \[ 1 , 0 , 0 ; 0 , 1 , 0 ; 0 , 0 , 1 \] matrix \[ , , ; , A^(-1) , ; , , \])"}
%[text] Note that if the inverse does not exist, it will not be possible to achieve this form. This is discussed in greater detail below.
%[text] ![](text:image:4911) **Example**. Solve for the inverse of $\\mathbf A${"altText":"A"} using row operations.
%[text] **Solution.** Move the slider forward to see the next step of the solution.
kstep = 1; %[control:slider:3533]{"position":[9,10]}
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf A = \n\\left(\\begin{array}{ccc}\n0.5 &  2 & 1\\\\\n1 & 2 & -1\\\\\n-1 & 0 & -4\n\\end{array}\\right)\n${"altText":"A = matrix \[ 0.5 , 2 , 1; 1 , 2 , -1; -1 , 0 , -4 \]"}
InvDialogue(kstep);
%[text] ![](text:image:67eb) **Exercise**. Verify by hand that $\\mathbf{A}^{-1}${"altText":"A^(-1)"} computed in the example above is the inverse of $\\mathbf A${"altText":"A"} (verify that $\\mathbf A^{-1} \\mathbf A${"altText":"A^(-1)A"} equals $\\mathbf I${"altText":"I"}).
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf A = \n\\left(\\begin{array}{ccc}\n0.5 &  2 & 1\\\\\n1 & 2 & -1\\\\\n-1 & 0 & -4\n\\end{array}\\right)\n${"altText":"A = matrix \[ 0.5 , 2 , 1; 1 , 2 , -1; -1 , 0 , -4 \]"},    $\\mathrm{\\mathbf A^{-1} =\\ }\\mathrm{}\\left(\\begin{array}{ccc}\n-1 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\\\\n\\frac{5}{8} & -\\frac{1}{8} & \\frac{3}{16}\\\\\n\\frac{1}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} & -\\frac{1}{8}\n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"A^(-1) = matrix \[ -1 , 1 , -(1)\/(2); (5)\/(8) , -(1)\/(8) , (3)\/(16); (1)\/(4) , -(1)\/(4) , -(1)\/(8) \]"}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_680a] ### **Formulas for inverses of square matrices**
%[text] The inverse of a 2x2 matrix 
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf A = \\left(\\begin{array}{cc}\na & b\\\\\nc & d\n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"A = matrix \[ a , b; c , d \]"}  is  $\\mathbf A^{-1} = \\frac{1}{ad - bc} \\left(\\begin{array}{cc}\nd & -b\\\\\n-c &a\n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"A^(-1) = (1)\/(ad - bc) matrix \[ d , -b; -c , a \]"}.
%[text] The inverse of a 3x3 matrix 
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf A =\n\\left(\\begin{array}{ccc}\na & b & c\\\\\nd & e & f\\\\\ng & h & i\n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"A = matrix \[ a , b , c; d , e , f; g , h , i \]"}   is   $\\mathbf A^{-1} =\n\\frac{1}{a\\,e\\,i-a\\,f\\,h-b\\,d\\,i+b\\,f\\,g+c\\,d\\,h-c\\,e\\,g}\n\\left(\\begin{array}{ccc}\ne\\,i-f\\,h & -b\\,i+c\\,h& b\\,f-c\\,e\\\\\n-d\\,i+f\\,g& a\\,i-c\\,g & -a\\,f+c\\,d\\\\\nd\\,h-e\\,g & -a\\,h+b\\,g & a\\,e-b\\,d\n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"A^(-1) = (1)\/(a e i-a f h-b d i+b f g+c d h-c e g) matrix \[ e i-f h , -b i+c h, b f-c e; -d i+f g, a i-c g , -a f+c d; d h-e g , -a h+b g , a e-b d \]"}.
%[text] For larger matrices, the formulas become quite complicated.
Show4x4 = false; %[control:checkbox:5793]{"position":[11,16]}
if(Show4x4)
    A = sym("a",[4,4])
    inv(A)
    disp("If this makes you feel dizzy, you can uncheck the box.")
end
%[text] ![](text:image:6b4c) **Try** 
%[text] - Use row-reduction to derive the general inverse of the 2x2 matrix $\\mathbf A = \\left(\\begin{array}{cc}\na & b\\\\\nc & d\n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"A = matrix \[ a , b; c , d \]"} by hand.
%[text] - Verify the formula for the 2x2 matrix inverse by computing the matrix product $\\mathbf A^{-1} \\mathbf A${"altText":"A^(-1)A"} by hand. \
%[text] ![](text:image:376a) **Reflect**
%[text] - For many square matrices, the inverse does not exist. The existence of the general formula makes this fact a little surprising. Review the formulas for the inverses of the 2x2 and 3x3 matrices shown above. What condition would lead to an undefined inverse? \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0295] ## **Invertibility of square matrices**
%[text] **Definition**. A $n\\times n${"altText":"n times n"} matrix is said to be **invertible** if its inverse exists.
%[text] Examining the formula for the matrix inverse can offer insight into the invertibility of a matrix. Consider the 2x2 matrix inverse:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf A^{-1} = \\frac{1}{ad - bc} \\left(\\begin{array}{cc}\nd & -b\\\\\n-c &a\n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"A^(-1) = (1)\/(ad - bc) matrix \[ d , -b; -c , a \]"}
%[text] If the denominator of the coefficient term is zero, i.e., $ad - bc = 0${"altText":"ad - bc = 0"}, the matrix inverse will not be defined (terms in the matrix will be either $\\frac{1}{0}${"altText":"(1)\/(0)"} or $\\frac{0}{0}${"altText":"(0)\/(0)"}).  
%[text] Similarly, in the $3 \\times 3${"altText":"3 times 3"} case
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf A^{-1} =\n\\frac{1}{a\\,e\\,i-a\\,f\\,h-b\\,d\\,i+b\\,f\\,g+c\\,d\\,h-c\\,e\\,g}\n\\left(\\begin{array}{ccc}\ne\\,i-f\\,h & -b\\,i+c\\,h& b\\,f-c\\,e\\\\\n-d\\,i+f\\,g& a\\,i-c\\,g & -a\\,f+c\\,d\\\\\nd\\,h-e\\,g & -a\\,h+b\\,g & a\\,e-b\\,d\n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"A^(-1) = (1)\/(a e i-a f h-b d i+b f g+c d h-c e g) matrix \[ e i-f h , -b i+c h, b f-c e; -d i+f g, a i-c g , -a f+c d; d h-e g , -a h+b g , a e-b d \]"}.
%[text] the matrix inverse will be undefined if $a\\,e\\,i-a\\,f\\,h-b\\,d\\,i+b\\,f\\,g+c\\,d\\,h-c\\,e\\,g = 0${"altText":"a e i-a f h-b d i+b f g+c d h-c e g = 0"}. 
%[text] ![](text:image:5762) **Reflect**
%[text] - Does a similar condition exist for the 4x4 matrix inverse? \
Show4x4 = false; %[control:checkbox:7e8e]{"position":[11,16]}
if(Show4x4)
    A = sym("a",[4 4])
    inv(A)
    disp("If this makes you feel dizzy, you can uncheck the box.")
end
