%[text] %[text:anchor:H_1d97] ### Matrix determinant
%[text] The determinant is a scalar value that characterizes the invertibility of a square ($n \\times n${"altText":"n times n"}) matrix. 
%[text] - The determinant is not defined for non-square matrices.
%[text] - Vertical lines $| \\cdot |${"altText":"| dot |"} denote the determinant of a matrix.  \
%[text] **Determinant of a 2x2 matrix**
%[text] The determinant of a 2x2 matrix is $|\\mathbf{A}| = \\left| \\matrix{a & b \\cr c & d } \\right| = ad - bc${"altText":"|A| = | matrix \[a , b ; c , d \] | = ad - bc"}.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![2x2 determinant](text:image:946a)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *You can think of the determinant of a 2x2 matrix as subtracting the product of the off-diagonal elements from the product of the diagonal elements.*
%[text] 
%[text] The determinants of larger matrices can be computed algorithmically using determinants of submatrices. This process is known as the Laplace expansion. 
%[text] **Determinant of a 3x3 matrix**
%[text] You can use the Laplace expansion to compute the determinant of a 3x3 matrix using 2x2 submatrices:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $|\\mathbf{A}|= \\left| \\matrix{a & b & c \\cr d & e & f \\cr g & h & i } \\right| = a \\left| \\matrix{ e & f \\cr h & i } \\right| - b \\left| \\matrix{ d & f \\cr g & i } \\right|  +c \\left| \\matrix{ d & e \\cr g & h } \\right|${"altText":"|A|= | matrix \[a , b , c ; d , e , f ; g , h , i \] | = a | matrix \[ e , f ; h , i \] | - b | matrix \[ d , f ; g , i \] | +c | matrix \[ d , e ; g , h \] |"}
%[text] You can compute the determinants of the 2x2 submatrices using the formula defined above.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Laplace expansion](text:image:8623)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Schematic of the Laplace expansion for a 3x3 matrix. Visualize selecting one element from the first row and then selecting the submatrix from the other rows and columns. Also watch out for the sign changes in the formula.*
%[text] Expanding the determinants of the submatrices yields the 3x3 determinant formula:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $|\\mathbf{A}|= a\\,e\\,i-a\\,f\\,h-b\\,d\\,i+b\\,f\\,g+c\\,d\\,h-c\\,e\\,g${"altText":"|A|= a e i-a f h-b d i+b f g+c d h-c e g"}
%[text] You can similarly expand larger matrices to break them down in terms of smaller matrices. However, for such cases, numerical software is typically used.
%[text] ![](text:image:485e) **Example**. Compute the determinant of $A${"altText":"A"} using the Laplace expansion. You can adjust the size.
n = 3;  %[control:slider:6262]{"position":[5,6]}
  %[control:button:8bfb]{"position":[1,2]}
A = sym(randi(9,n,n));
displayFormula(["'A = '", "A"])
%%
ShowSolutiond = false; %[control:checkbox:4a21]{"position":[17,22]}
if(ShowSolutiond)
    DetA = det(A)
end
%%
%[text] **Determinant invertibility condition.** A square $n\\times n${"altText":"n times n"} matrix is invertible if and only if its **determinant** is nonzero.
%[text] - Note that the invertibility of a square matrix can be related to many other matrix properties outside the current discussion's scope. \
%[text] **Understanding the determinant invertibility condition**
%[text] Consider the 2x2 determinant: $ad - bc${"altText":"ad - bc"}. This is the same expression as that found in the denominator of the matrix inverse. This implies that, for the inverse to be defined, $| \\mathbf A| = ad - bc${"altText":"| A| = ad - bc"} must be nonzero.  You can also write the formula for the inverse as:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf A^{-1} = \\frac{1}{|\\mathbf A|} \\left(\\begin{array}{cc}\nd & -b\\\\\n-c &a\n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"A^(-1) = (1)\/(|A|) matrix \[ d , -b; -c , a \]"}. 
%[text] The concept extends to higher-dimensional matrices. In the 3x3 case, the determinant is $|\\mathbf A| = a\\,e\\,i-a\\,f\\,h-b\\,d\\,i+b\\,f\\,g+c\\,d\\,h-c\\,e\\,g${"altText":"|A| = a e i-a f h-b d i+b f g+c d h-c e g"}. So, the inverse formula is
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf A^{-1} =\n\\frac{1}{|\\mathbf A|}\n\\left(\\begin{array}{ccc}\ne\\,i-f\\,h & -b\\,i+c\\,h& b\\,f-c\\,e\\\\\n-d\\,i+f\\,g& a\\,i-c\\,g & -a\\,f+c\\,d\\\\\nd\\,h-e\\,g & -a\\,h+b\\,g & a\\,e-b\\,d\n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"A^(-1) = (1)\/(|A|) matrix \[ e i-f h , -b i+c h, b f-c e; -d i+f g, a i-c g , -a f+c d; d h-e g , -a h+b g , a e-b d \]"},
%[text] which is well defined if and only if $|\\mathbf A| \\neq 0${"altText":"|A| != 0"}.
%[text] ![](text:image:6bde) **Exercise**. 
%[text] (a) Determine if the matrices are invertible.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\mathbf A = \n \\left(\\begin{array}{ccc}\n1 & 2 & 3\\\\\n4 & 5 & 6\\\\\n8 & 9 & 10\n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"A = matrix \[ 1 , 2 , 3; 4 , 5 , 6; 8 , 9 , 10 \]"} $\\mathbf{B} = \n \\left(\\begin{array}{ccc}\n1 & 1 & 1\\\\\n1 & 0 & 0\\\\\n0 & 0 & 1\n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"B = matrix \[ 1 , 1 , 1; 1 , 0 , 0; 0 , 0 , 1 \]"} $\\mathbf{C} = \n\\left(\\begin{array}{ccc}\n1 & 0.5 & -1\\\\\n0 & 2 & 1\\\\\n-4 & 1 & 1\n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"C = matrix \[ 1 , 0.5 , -1; 0 , 2 , 1; -4 , 1 , 1 \]"} 
ShowSolution = false; %[control:checkbox:6f73]{"position":[16,21]}
As = [1 2 3; 4 5 6; 8 9 10];
Bs = [1 1 1; 1 0 0; 0 0 1];
Cs = [1 0.5 -1; 0 2 1; -4 1 1];
if(ShowSolution)
    disp("|A| = " + string(det(sym(As))) + " so the matrix is not invertible.")
    disp("|B| = " + string(det(sym(Bs))) + " so the matrix is invertible.")
    disp("|C| = " + string(det(sym(Cs))) + " so the matrix is invertible.")
end
