%[text] %[text:anchor:H_5717] ## **Working with linear systems in MATLAB**
%[text] Small systems of equations are not difficult to solve by hand using row reduction or inverses. But what happens if the system has tens, hundreds, or even thousands of variables? 
%[text] Adjust the slider to select the size of the system $\\mathbf{Ax} = \\mathbf{b}${"altText":"Ax=b"}:
n = 15; %[control:slider:8fd1]{"position":[5,7]}
A = randi(20,[n n])-10;
b = randi(20,[n 1])-10;
x = sym("x",[n 1]);
displayFormula([char(sym(A)),char(sym(x)),"' = '",char(sym(b))]);
%[text] Solving this system by hand isn't a viable option. You'll want to use numerical software to solve your system. In MATLAB, you can define a linear system $\\mathbf{Ax} = \\mathbf{b}${"altText":"Ax=b"} in terms of matrices and vectors and solve the system using built-in functions. There are two approaches: numeric and symbolic. In this section, you will explore both approaches. In general, the same basic functions for working with matrices (such as `inv` and `det`) work for both symbolic and numeric matrices. In this section, the solution vector is expressed as:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf{x} = \\left( \\matrix{ x\_1 \\cr x\_2 \\cr \\vdots \\cr x\_n} \\right)${"altText":"x = ( matrix \[ x\_1 ; x\_2 ; ... ; x\_n\] )"}.
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:25c5) **Exercise**. In this exercise, you will analyze and solve a linear system using numeric matrices. Perform the tasks using MATLAB commands in the space provided.
%[text] ![](text:image:9d7a) **Task 1.** Determine if the system $\\mathbf{Ax} = \\mathbf{b}${"altText":"Ax=b"} defined above has a unique solution. The matrix $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} is stored in the variable `A` and the vector $\\mathbf{b}${"altText":"b"} is stored in `b`.
%[text] **Hint**: use the MATLAB determinant function: [`det`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/det.html).
  %[control:editfield:3e5a]{"position":[1,2]}
  %[control:button:279e]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
answer = 0; %[control:dropdown:0eef]{"position":[10,11]}
IsEqualCheck(answer,det(A)~=0);
  %[control:button:2190]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:04d3) **Task 2.** Compute the inverse of `A`.
InvA = [ ] %[control:editfield:4147]{"position":[9,10]}
  %[control:button:6a36]{"position":[1,2]}
IsEqualCheck(inv(A),InvA);
%[text] **Hint**: [`inv`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/inv.html).
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:71ab) **Task 3.** Use the inverse InvA to compute the solution to $\\mathbf{Ax} = \\mathbf{b}${"altText":"Ax=b"}.
xSoln1 = [ ] %[control:editfield:7737]{"position":[11,12]}
  %[control:button:2679]{"position":[1,2]}
IsEqualCheck(xSoln1,inv(A)*b);
%%
%[text] There are other (and often better) ways to solve a linear system in MATLAB than the inverse. The most common method is using the [backslash operator](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/double.mldivide.html) (`\`). Conceptually, this operator is equivalent to a "left divide". Thus, using this operator, the solution to a linear system 
%[text]  `Ax = b`
%[text] is found by "left-dividing" `b` by `A`. In quasi-mathematical terms:
%[text]  `A\Ax = A\b`
%[text]  `x = A\b`
%[text] ![](text:image:9e6c) **Task 4.** Use the left divide operator to solve the linear system $\\mathbf{Ax} = \\mathbf{b}${"altText":"Ax=b"}.
xSoln2 = [ ]; %[control:editfield:160d]{"position":[11,12]}
  %[control:button:5691]{"position":[1,2]}
xSoln2
IsEqualCheck(xSoln2,A\b);
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:7e1f) **Task 5.** Is the solution computed using the inverse the same as the solution computed using the backslash operator? To find out, try taking the differences of the two solutions: `xSoln1` and `xSoln2`.
  %[control:editfield:79fd]{"position":[1,2]}
  %[control:button:398a]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] ![](text:image:759b) **Reflect**
%[text] - Is either solution exact?
%[text] - In general, numeric solutions computed with different algorithms will be different. What causes these differences?
%[text] - For small matrices, the method is typically not important. However, for large matrices, the inverse is not the preferred method as it can lead to large numerical errors. \
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:70d1) **Exercise**. In this exercise, you will analyze and solve a linear system using symbolic variables. Perform the tasks using MATLAB commands in the space provided.
%[text] ![](text:image:290e) **Task 1.** Several symbolic variables are defined below. Define a matrix $\\mathbf{C}${"altText":"C"} and a constant vector $\\mathbf{d}${"altText":"d"}. Choose any values for $\\mathbf{C}${"altText":"C"} and $\\mathbf{d}${"altText":"d"} that satisfy:
%[text] - $\\mathbf{C}${"altText":"C"} is an $n \\times n${"altText":"n times n"} numeric matrix
%[text] - $\\mathbf{d}${"altText":"d"} is an $n${"altText":"n"}-element numeric column vector
%[text] - both $\\mathbf{C}${"altText":"C"} and $\\mathbf{d}${"altText":"d"} have some symbolic elements and some numeric elements \
%[text] You can decide how large to make the matrices (e.g., $n = 3${"altText":"n=3"}).
syms a b c r p q  %[control:editfield:6f48]{"position":[1,18]}
C = [ ] %[control:editfield:530e]{"position":[6,7]}
d = [ ] %[control:editfield:17fb]{"position":[6,7]}
  %[control:button:795e]{"position":[1,2]}
if(size(d,1)~=size(C,1))
    warning("d should have the same number of rows as C!")
end
if(size(C,2)~=size(C,1))
    warning("C should be square!")
end
if(size(d,2)>1)
    warning("d should be a column vector!")
end
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:4d59) **Task 2.** The symbolic variable vector $\\mathbf{x}${"altText":"x"} is defined below. Use it to compute the algebraic form of the linear system $\\mathbf{Cx} = \\mathbf{d}${"altText":"Cx=d"}.
x = sym("x",[length(d),1])
LinearSystem = [ ] %[control:editfield:44b7]{"position":[17,18]}
  %[control:button:8346]{"position":[1,2]}
if isempty(LinearSystem)
    disp("That is the default. Please set up the linear system.")
else
    IsEqualCheck(LinearSystem,C*x==d);
end
%[text] ![](text:image:4926) **Pro-tip.** For symbolic equality, use [`==`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/symbolic/sym.eq.html). 
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:3250) **Task 3.** What condition on the constants will make the system $\\mathbf{Cx} = \\mathbf{d}${"altText":"Cx=d"} have a unique solution? Type your condition in the space provided below using symbolic variables and operators.
condition = [ ] %[control:editfield:9c94]{"position":[14,15]}
  %[control:button:5be4]{"position":[1,2]}
if isempty(condition)
    disp("That is the default. Please enter a condition for the equation C*x=d to have a unique solution.")
else
    IsEqualCheck(simplify(condition), simplify(det(C)~=0));
end
%[text] **Hint**: [`det`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/det.html) and the symbolic inequality operator: [~=](https://www.mathworks.com/help/symbolic/sym.ne.html)`.`
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:540e) **Task 4.** Compute $\\mathbf{C}^{-1}${"altText":"C^(-1)"}.
Cinv = [ ]; %[control:editfield:3b6d]{"position":[9,10]}
  %[control:button:6df4]{"position":[1,2]}
Cinv
IsEqualCheck(Cinv,inv(C));
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:75b3) **Task 5.** The inverse that you computed is stored in a variable `Cinv`. Use it to compute the solution $\\mathbf{x}${"altText":"x"} to the linear system $\\mathbf{Cx} = \\mathbf{d}${"altText":"Cx=d"}.
